# Vercel Web Analytics Installation Report

## Summary
Successfully installed and configured Vercel Web Analytics for the static HTML Volleyball game project.

## Changes Made

### Modified Files:
- **index.html** - Added Vercel Web Analytics scripts in the `<head>` section

## Implementation Details

### Framework Detection
This project is a static HTML/JavaScript application (no framework detected), so I followed the **Static HTML** installation instructions from the official Vercel documentation.

### What Was Added
Added two script tags to the `<head>` section of `index.html`:

1. **Analytics Initialization Script**: Initializes the `window.va` function and queue for tracking
2. **Deferred Analytics Script**: Loads the Vercel analytics tracking script with defer attribute for optimal performance

```html
<!-- Vercel Web Analytics -->
<script>
    window.va = window.va || function () { (window.vaq = window.vaq || []).push(arguments); };
</script>
<script defer src="/_vercel/insights/script.js"></script>
```

### Location
The analytics scripts were placed immediately after the meta tags and before the styles section to ensure:
- Early initialization of the analytics queue
- Deferred loading to not block page rendering
- Proper tracking of page views from the start

## Documentation Source
All implementation steps were based on the latest official Vercel documentation fetched from:
https://vercel.com/docs/analytics/quickstart

## Verification Steps
After deployment to Vercel, analytics can be verified by:
1. Visiting the deployed site
2. Opening browser DevTools > Network tab
3. Looking for a Fetch/XHR request to `/<unique-path>/view`

## Notes
- No package.json exists in this project (pure static HTML), so no package installation was required
- The implementation is minimal and non-intrusive, preserving all existing functionality
- Analytics will only become active once the site is deployed to Vercel with analytics enabled in the dashboard
- The deferred loading ensures no performance impact on the game
